XFER(1)                      User Commands

NAME
    xfer - transfer files between the guest and the host

SYNOPSIS
    xfer put [-h HOST] [-p PORT] PATH...
    xfer get [-h HOST] [-p PORT] [-o DIR] NAME...
    xfer ls [-h HOST] [-p PORT] [DIR]
    xfer serve [-p PORT] [DIR]

DESCRIPTION
    Copies files and directories over TCP. HOST defaults to 10.0.2.2,
    the host machine as seen from QEMU user networking, and PORT to 9100,
    so "xfer put notes.txt" in the guest sends a file to a host running
    "tools/xfer.py serve", "xfer get build.tar" fetches one, and "xfer ls"
    lists the served directory. A directory is transferred file by file
    under its name. Every transfer carries a CRC32 checked by both ends.

    serve makes DIR (default the current directory) available; the host
    reaches it through the port forward that "make run --nic user" sets
    up on port 9100, with "tools/xfer.py put FILE". Paths in requests are
    relative and may not contain "..". A connection without progress for
    30 seconds is dropped. While a transfer runs, every side whose standard
    error is a terminal (the client, the server's child and tools/xfer.py)
    prints the bytes done and the throughput once per second; a summary
    line follows when the transfer completes.

OPTIONS
    -h HOST    server address
    -p PORT    server port
    -o DIR     directory that get writes into (default .)

EXAMPLES
    xfer put /home/report.txt
    xfer get -o /tmp photos
    xfer serve /home

SEE ALSO
    nc(1), http(1), net(1), index(7)
